# Zephyr Circuit — released 2026-08-29 (Pacific)

**This is a clock-based release, not a merit-based one.** The game reached its
forced release Saturday under the arcade's two-week cadence: started
2026-08-17, released at 12 days old. STEP 4's quality gate — every discipline's
critic passing a blind side-by-side comparison, plus an independent shipping
judge — was **never met**. No shipping judge was run, because a shipping judge
is only meaningful once the critics pass, and they did not.

What was verified on release night is that the game is *not broken*. What is
recorded below is everything that is still short of AAA.

## Release-blocker gate — PASS

The forced-release bar is narrow: a crash, console errors, an unreadable HUD,
broken touch controls, or a race that cannot be completed. All five were
checked on the released build.

* **Test suite green.** 9 suites, 237 tests, run three times tonight — before
  the HUD fix, after it, and before the featured-game commit.
* **All three circuits complete end to end.** A deterministic AI cup smoke ran
  the real race loop through Gullwing Bay (133s), Thermal Spire (128s) and
  Cirrus Run (149s). Every round reached the results state with **8/8 karts
  finishing and zero falls**, the cup ended in `phase: cup` with a 3,434 cup
  score and a 1,200 championship bonus.
* **Six-viewport sweep, PNGs actually read.** 320x568, 390x844, 844x390,
  768x1024, 1440x900 and 3840x2160 all reported the race running with 8 karts,
  exact viewport dimensions, matching scroll width/height and no horizontal
  scrollbar. Each PNG was opened and inspected, not just the report.
* **Console clean.** Only the two documented sandbox artefacts: the blocked
  Google tag request and a swiftshader `GPU stall due to ReadPixels` warning.
  Every other game on the site produces the same tag failure.
* **Touch controls present and correct** at 320x568, 390x844, 844x390 and
  768x1024: steering pads bottom-left/right, brake and drift under the right
  thumb, throttle automatic.

### The one blocker found and fixed tonight

At **844x390 (phone landscape)** the race clock overlapped the lap card: the
landscape rule parked `.zc-timing` at `right: 112px`, but `.zc-lap` is a
`1fr auto` grid whose real width is about 208px, so "LAP" clipped to "AP" and
"Gullwing Bay" clipped to "Bay". Fixed by bounding `.zc-lap` to a deterministic
164px inside that media query and moving the clock clear of it. Only 844x390
matches `max-height: 460px and (orientation: landscape)`, so no other viewport
changed. Re-shot and visually confirmed.

## Critic record — the honest version

No discipline ever recorded a passing blind side-by-side verdict. These are the
final standing verdicts carried into release:

| discipline | reference | verdict |
|---|---|---|
| FX (`render/fx.js`) | Mario Kart 8 Deluxe | **FAIL** — three blind rounds, all lost |
| Track / environment | Art of Rally | **FAIL** |
| Kart models | Mario Kart 8 Deluxe | **FAIL** |
| Audio | Mario Kart 8 Deluxe | **FAIL / not AAA-cleared** |
| HUD / touch | Mario Kart 8 Deluxe | **FAIL** |
| UI (menus, results, cup) | — | **UNJUDGED** |
| Shipping judge | — | **NOT RUN** |

A caveat recorded by an earlier run and worth repeating: on at least one night
reference imagery could not be fetched from the sandbox, so a verdict from that
night reflects what was actually compared, which was less than a true blind
side-by-side.

## Carried polish debt

**FX** — the exact list, unchanged across three failed critic rounds: localize
and reduce the contact flash so it stops blowing out; preserve kart silhouettes
through the effect; author distinct wheel-attached drift-spark tiers rather
than detached sparks; add a clearly directional, saturated rear exhaust plume
that reads from the player kart.

**Track / environment** — no reliably readable hero structure in the chase
composition; too much empty asphalt; clipped dark foliage; sparse, muddy
supporting dressing. Thermal Spire's landmark pass (spire, caldera, basalt
shelves, lava bands, cap) improved the single landmark without fixing the
composition. Track polish is uneven between the three circuits.

**Kart models** — tire contact and surface detail lose to the reference.

**Audio** — the thinnest discipline in the game, never brought near the bar. It
was a ~110-line placeholder for most of development and no critic has cleared
it.

**HUD / UI** — hierarchy and all-background robustness below the bar; the
mobile touch stack is dense (item, speed, brake and drift compete for the same
corner in portrait). At 3840x2160 the HUD does not scale with the viewport and
reads very small — legible, but not composed for 4K. Menu, results and cup
screens were never judged at all. Camera and UI "drama" — the staging that
makes a kart racer feel like an event — is absent.

**Validation** — no real-GPU verification exists. This sandbox has no GPU, so
every frame rate figure came from swiftshader and shadow quality has never been
seen on real hardware. `renderer.info` draw-call and triangle counts are
meaningless with the EffectComposer in the pipeline. **Whether this game holds
60fps on a real device is unknown.**

## What is genuinely good

Recorded so the debt list above is not read as the whole picture. The logic
half is well tested and the invariants that matter are defended: the circuit
provably never crosses itself, AI skill ratings predict finishing order,
drifting is measurably faster than not drifting, physics is a fixed timestep
with no `Math.random`. The golden-hour low-poly look reads cleanly at desktop
and tablet sizes, all art is procedural, nothing is fetched from a third party,
and the whole three.js/font stack is vendored into the repo.

## If someone picks this up again

Start with **audio** and **UI/menus** — they are the two disciplines that were
never really attempted, so they have the most headroom per hour. Then the FX
debt list above, which is specific enough to work from directly. Do the first
real-GPU pass before trusting any performance claim in this file.
